{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,700p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/b9e38dc0.py && sed -n '1,420p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/305b1341.py && sed -n '1,360p' /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/320afe60.py && sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed56627177/9/des.md && sed -n '1,240p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed56627177/9/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"import numpy as np\n\nfrom arc_utils import neighbors4, neighbors8, components_mask, get_bg\nimport random\n\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Fill the region enclosed by a wall sprite and radiate color outward through its opening.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - bg = most common color. Wall = most frequent non-bg color, forming a partial enclosure (e.g. U-shape).\n    - A fill-color pixel sits inside the enclosed area. Other non-bg pixels may act as obstacles.\n    Rule:\n    - Flood-fill the enclosed area with the fill color, radiating outward through the wall's open end.\n    - Obstacles cast \"shadows\" along the radiate direction, blocking fill behind them.\n\n    Key insight: The wall endpoints define the opening direction; fill radiates outward through it, and obstacles block propagation along that same axis.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.array(grid, copy=True)\n    h, w = g.shape\n\n    vals = np.unique(g)\n    bg = get_bg(g)\n\n    non_bg = [int(v) for v in vals if int(v) != bg]\n    assert non_bg, \"expected at least one non-background color\"\n\n    # Wall: most frequent non-background color.\n    wall = max(non_bg, key=lambda c: int((g == c).sum()))\n\n    wall_mask = g == wall\n\n    # Wall connected components (8-neigh).\n    comps = [set((int(r), int(c)) for r, c in pts) for pts in components_mask(wall_mask, connectivity=8)]\n    assert comps, \"expected at least one wall connected component\"\n\n    def sgn(x: int) -> int:\n        return (x > 0) - (x < 0)\n\n    other_colors = [c for c in non_bg if c != wall]\n    assert other_colors, \"expected at least one non-wall color (fill color)\"\n\n    # Pick fill color as the non-wall color closest (Manhattan distance) to the wall pixels.\n    wall_points = [p for comp in comps for p in comp]\n\n    def min_dist_to_wall(color: int) -> tuple[int, int]:\n        pts = list(zip(*np.where(g == color)))\n        pts = [(int(r), int(c)) for r, c in pts]\n        best_d = 10**9\n        for r, c in pts:\n            for wr, wc in wall_points:\n                d = abs(r - wr) + abs(c - wc)\n                if d < best_d:\n                    best_d = d\n                    if best_d == 0:\n                        return 0, len(pts)\n        return best_d, len(pts)\n\n    fill = min(other_colors, key=lambda c: (min_dist_to_wall(c)[0], min_dist_to_wall(c)[1]))\n\n    fill_pos = list(zip(*np.where(g == fill)))\n    fill_pos = [(int(r), int(c)) for r, c in fill_pos]\n    if not fill_pos:\n        return g\n\n    # Choose the closest wall component to the fill seed (ties: larger component).\n    def comp_min_dist(comp: set[tuple[int, int]]) -> int:\n        best_d = 10**9\n        for r, c in fill_pos:\n            for wr, wc in comp:\n                d = abs(r - wr) + abs(c - wc)\n                if d < best_d:\n                    best_d = d\n                    if best_d == 0:\n                        return 0\n        return best_d\n\n    wall_comp = min(comps, key=lambda comp: (comp_min_dist(comp), -len(comp)))\n\n    comp_mask = np.zeros_like(wall_mask, dtype=bool)\n    for r, c in wall_comp:\n        comp_mask[r, c] = True\n\n    def comp_neighbors(r: int, c: int) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n        out = []\n        for rr, cc in neighbors8(r, c):\n            if 0 <= rr < h and 0 <= cc < w and comp_mask[rr, cc]:\n                out.append((rr, cc))\n        return out\n\n    endpoints: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n    for r, c in wall_comp:\n        if len(comp_neighbors(r, c)) == 1:\n            endpoints.append((r, c))\n\n    assert endpoints, \"expected wall component to have endpoints (degree-1 pixels)\"\n\n    def outward_dir(ep: tuple[int, int]) -> tuple[tuple[int, int], bool]:\n        n1 = comp_neighbors(*ep)[0]\n        d1 = (sgn(n1[0] - ep[0]), sgn(n1[1] - ep[1]))  # inward\n        d_use = d1\n        is_12 = False\n        n2s = [p for p in comp_neighbors(*n1) if p != ep]\n        if n2s:\n            n2 = n2s[0]\n            d2 = (sgn(n2[0] - n1[0]), sgn(n2[1] - n1[1]))\n            if (d1[0] == 0) ^ (d1[1] == 0):\n                if d2[0] != 0 and d2[1] != 0:\n                    d_use = d2\n                    is_12 = True\n        return (-d_use[0], -d_use[1]), is_12\n\n    out_info = [outward_dir(ep) for ep in endpoints]\n    out_dirs = [d for d, _ in out_info]\n    tip_12 = [f for _, f in out_info]\n\n    dr_sum = sum(d[0] for d in out_dirs)\n    dc_sum = sum(d[1] for d in out_dirs)\n    rad = (sgn(dr_sum), sgn(dc_sum))\n\n    barrier = comp_mask.copy()\n\n    def add_ray(start: tuple[int, int], d: tuple[int, int], skip: int = 0) -> None:\n        rr, cc = start\n        for _ in range(skip):\n            rr += d[0]\n            cc += d[1]\n        while True:\n            rr += d[0]\n            cc += d[1]\n            if not (0 <= rr < h and 0 <= cc < w):\n                break\n            barrier[rr, cc] = True\n\n    def add_ray_12(start: tuple[int, int], d: tuple[int, int]) -> None:\n        rr, cc = start\n        step = 0\n        while True:\n            step += 1\n            if step % 2 == 1:\n                rr += d[0]; cc += d[1]\n            else:\n                if rad[0] != 0:\n                    rr += d[0]\n                else:\n                    cc += d[1]\n            if not (0 <= rr < h and 0 <= cc < w):\n                break\n            barrier[rr, cc] = True\n\n    for ep, d, is_12 in zip(endpoints, out_dirs, tip_12):\n        if is_12:\n            add_ray_12(ep, d)\n        else:\n            add_ray(ep, d, skip=0)\n\n    passable = (~barrier) & ((g == bg) | (g == fill))\n    q = fill_pos[:]\n    seen = np.zeros_like(passable, dtype=bool)\n    for r, c in q:\n        if passable[r, c]:\n            seen[r, c] = True\n    while q:\n        r, c = q.pop()\n        for rr, cc in neighbors4(r, c):\n            if 0 <= rr < h and 0 <= cc < w and passable[rr, cc] and not seen[rr, cc]:\n                seen[rr, cc] = True\n                q.append((rr, cc))\n\n    filled_mask = (seen & (g == bg))\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    out[filled_mask] = fill\n\n    # Shadow behind obstacles (non-bg, non-fill, non-wall) along radiate direction.\n    obstacles = (g != bg) & (g != fill) & (g != wall)\n\n    def shadow_process(coords: list[tuple[int, int]]) -> None:\n        started = False\n        blocked = False\n        for r, c in coords:\n            if filled_mask[r, c]:\n                started = True\n            if started and obstacles[r, c]:\n                blocked = True\n                continue\n            if blocked and filled_mask[r, c]:\n                out[r, c] = bg\n                filled_mask[r, c] = False\n\n    if rad != (0, 0):\n        dr, dc = rad\n        if dr != 0 and dc == 0:\n            for c in range(w):\n                rows = range(h) if dr > 0 else range(h - 1, -1, -1)\n                shadow_process([(r, c) for r in rows])\n        elif dc != 0 and dr == 0:\n            for r in range(h):\n                cols = range(w) if dc > 0 else range(w - 1, -1, -1)\n                shadow_process([(r, c) for c in cols])\n        else:\n            # Diagonal scanlines\n            if dr == dc:\n                # r - c = const\n                for k in range(-(w - 1), h):\n                    coords = [(r, r - k) for r in range(h) if 0 <= r - k < w]\n                    coords.sort(key=lambda rc: rc[0], reverse=(dr < 0))\n                    shadow_process(coords)\n            else:\n                # r + c = const\n                for k in range(h + w - 1):\n                    coords = [(r, k - r) for r in range(h) if 0 <= k - r < w]\n                    coords.sort(key=lambda rc: rc[0], reverse=(dr < 0))\n                    shadow_process(coords)\n\n    return out\n\n# --------------- generator (o5x) ---------------\n\nfrom scipy.ndimage import distance_transform_cdt\n\n# -- Configuration constants --------------------------------------------------\n# Canvas size bounds\nGRID_H_VERT = (11, 18)       # height range when radiate_dir is vertical\nGRID_H_HORIZ = (8, 18)       # height range when radiate_dir is horizontal\nGRID_W = (15, 19)             # width range (all directions)\n\n# Wall geometry\nWALL_PIXELS_MIN = 17          # minimum wall pixel count\nWALL_PIXELS_MAX = 25          # maximum wall pixel count\nDEPTH_VERT = (5, 10)          # arm depth range for vertical radiate\nDEPTH_HORIZ = (6, 8)          # arm depth range for horizontal radiate\nGAP_VERT = (6, 7)             # endpoint gap for vertical radiate\nGAP_HORIZ = (5, 7)            # endpoint gap for horizontal radiate\nTIP_12_PROB = 0.5             # probability of 1:2 arm-angle mode (when eligible)\n\n# Placement margins\nOPEN_END_MARGIN = 3           # min cells from open end to radiate grid edge\n\n# Fill seed\nSEED_SIZE_WEIGHTS = [1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]  # weighted choices for blob size\nSEED_DEPTH_LOOKBACK = 3       # seed must be within this many rows of closed end\n\n# Obstacles\nMAX_OBSTACLES = 5             # maximum obstacle pixel count\nOBSTACLE_IN_FILL_PROB = 0.65  # probability of forcing at least one obstacle in fill zone\nOBSTACLE_MIN_SEP = 2          # Chebyshev distance between obstacles (> this value)\n\n# Validation\nMIN_NEW_FILL = 20             # minimum newly-filled pixels for a valid output\nPALETTE_SIZE = (3, 5)         # number of distinct colors (bg + wall + fill + obstacle(s))\nGEN_RETRIES = 300             # max attempts in generate_input\n\n\ndef _degree1_pixels(points):\n    \"\"\"Return degree-1 pixels (exactly one 8-neighbor in the set).\"\"\"\n    pts = set(points)\n    return [(r, c) for r, c in pts\n            if sum((rr, cc) in pts for rr, cc in neighbors8(r, c)) == 1]\n\n\ndef _make_arm(depth, start_v, target_v, extra_steps, tip_12=False):\n    \"\"\"Build one zigzag arm from (0, start_v) toward (depth, target_v).\"\"\"\n    sign = 1 if target_v > start_v else -1\n    n_side_steps = abs(target_v - start_v)\n    chosen_rows = []\n    if n_side_steps:\n        if tip_12:\n            chosen_rows = [2 * (i + 1) for i in range(n_side_steps)]\n            extra_steps = 0\n        else:\n            chosen_rows = [1]\n            if n_side_steps > 1:\n                chosen_rows += random.sample(range(2, depth + 1), n_side_steps - 1)\n            chosen_rows.sort()\n\n    first_row = chosen_rows[0] if chosen_rows else 1\n    extra_rows = [r for r in chosen_rows if r != first_row]\n    random.shuffle(extra_rows)\n    orthogonal_rows = set(extra_rows[:extra_steps])\n\n    pts = [(0, start_v)]\n    u, v = 0, start_v\n    for row in range(1, depth + 1):\n        u += 1\n        if row in orthogonal_rows:\n            pts.append((u, v))\n            v += sign\n        elif row in chosen_rows:\n            v += sign\n        pts.append((u, v))\n    return pts\n\n\ndef _build_local_wall(rad, h, w):\n    \"\"\"Construct wall shape in local (u, v) coordinates. Returns (pts, depth, gap) or None.\"\"\"\n    if rad[0]:\n        max_depth = min(DEPTH_VERT[1], h - 6)\n        if max_depth < DEPTH_VERT[0]:\n            return None\n        depth = random.randint(DEPTH_VERT[0], max_depth)\n        gap = random.randint(*GAP_VERT)\n    else:\n        depth = random.randint(DEPTH_HORIZ[0], min(DEPTH_HORIZ[1], w - 6))\n        gap = random.randint(GAP_HORIZ[0], min(GAP_HORIZ[1], h - 1))\n\n    if gap == 5:\n        left_bottom, right_bottom = 2, 3\n    elif gap == 6:\n        left_bottom, right_bottom = 2, 4\n    else:\n        left_bottom, right_bottom = random.choice([(2, 5), (3, 4)])\n\n    bridge_extra = max(0, right_bottom - left_bottom - 1)\n    base_count = 2 * (depth + 1) + bridge_extra\n    max_extra_l = max(0, left_bottom - 1)\n    max_extra_r = max(0, gap - right_bottom - 1)\n    min_extra = max(0, WALL_PIXELS_MIN - base_count)\n    max_extra = min(max_extra_l + max_extra_r, WALL_PIXELS_MAX - base_count)\n    if min_extra > max_extra:\n        return None\n\n    extra = random.randint(min_extra, max_extra)\n    extra_l = random.randint(max(0, extra - max_extra_r), min(max_extra_l, extra))\n    extra_r = extra - extra_l\n\n    max_n_side = max(left_bottom, gap - right_bottom)\n    tip_12 = depth >= 2 * max_n_side and random.random() < TIP_12_PROB\n    left = _make_arm(depth, 0, left_bottom, extra_l, tip_12=tip_12)\n    right = _make_arm(depth, gap, right_bottom, extra_r, tip_12=tip_12)\n    pts = set(left) | set(right)\n    for v in range(left_bottom, right_bottom + 1):\n        pts.add((depth, v))\n\n    # Pad wall to minimum size by adding 8-connected pixels that preserve 2 endpoints\n    while len(pts) < WALL_PIXELS_MIN:\n        candidates = [\n            (u, v) for u in range(max(0, depth - 3), depth + 1)\n            for v in range(gap + 1) if (u, v) not in pts\n            and sum((uu, vv) in pts\n                    for uu in range(u - 1, u + 2)\n                    for vv in range(v - 1, v + 2)\n                    if (uu, vv) != (u, v)) >= 2\n        ]\n        random.shuffle(candidates)\n        added = False\n        for p in candidates:\n            trial = pts | {p}\n            if len(trial) <= WALL_PIXELS_MAX and len(_degree1_pixels(trial)) == 2:\n                pts = trial\n                added = True\n                break\n        if not added:\n            return None\n\n    if not (WALL_PIXELS_MIN <= len(pts) <= WALL_PIXELS_MAX) or len(_degree1_pixels(pts)) != 2:\n        return None\n    return pts, depth, gap\n\n\ndef _map_local(u, v, rad, anchor):\n    \"\"\"Map local (u, v) to grid (r, c) given radiate direction and anchor.\"\"\"\n    r0, c0 = anchor\n    if rad == (-1, 0):\n        return r0 + u, c0 + v\n    if rad == (1, 0):\n        return r0 - u, c0 + v\n    if rad == (0, -1):\n        return r0 + v, c0 + u\n    return r0 + v, c0 - u\n\n\ndef _place_anchor(rad, h, w, depth, gap):\n    \"\"\"Choose anchor so the wall fits within margins.\"\"\"\n    if rad == (-1, 0):\n        return random.randint(OPEN_END_MARGIN, h - depth - 3), random.randint(0, w - gap - 1)\n    if rad == (1, 0):\n        return random.randint(depth + 2, h - 4), random.randint(0, w - gap - 1)\n    if rad == (0, -1):\n        return random.randint(0, h - gap - 1), random.randint(OPEN_END_MARGIN, w - depth - 3)\n    return random.randint(0, h - gap - 1), random.randint(depth + 2, w - 4)\n\n\ndef _local_interior(wall, depth, gap):\n    \"\"\"Return bg cells strictly between the two wall arms in local coords.\"\"\"\n    by_row = {}\n    for u, v in wall:\n        by_row.setdefault(u, []).append(v)\n    interior = []\n    for u in range(1, depth):\n        if u not in by_row or len(by_row[u]) < 2:\n            continue\n        lo, hi = min(by_row[u]), max(by_row[u])\n        for v in range(lo + 1, hi):\n            if (u, v) not in wall:\n                interior.append((u, v))\n    return interior\n\n\ndef _grow_seed_blob(seed, interior_set):\n    \"\"\"Grow a small 4-connected blob from seed within interior cells.\"\"\"\n    blob = {seed}\n    target_size = random.choice(SEED_SIZE_WEIGHTS)\n    frontier = [seed]\n    while len(blob) < target_size and frontier:\n        p = frontier.pop(random.randrange(len(frontier)))\n        u, v = p\n        for q in random.sample([(u - 1, v), (u + 1, v), (u, v - 1), (u, v + 1)], 4):\n            if q in interior_set and q not in blob:\n                blob.add(q)\n                frontier.append(q)\n                if len(blob) >= target_size:\n                    break\n    return blob\n\n\ndef _obstacle_fits(pos, placed):\n    \"\"\"Check that pos is at least Chebyshev OBSTACLE_MIN_SEP from all placed obstacles.\"\"\"\n    r, c = pos\n    return all(max(abs(r - rr), abs(c - cc)) >= OBSTACLE_MIN_SEP for rr, cc in placed)\n\n\ndef _find_obstacle_candidates(grid, bg, wall_color, nominal_fill_zone):\n    \"\"\"Find bg cells far enough from wall for obstacle placement, using distance transform.\"\"\"\n    wall_mask = grid == wall_color\n    # distance_transform_cdt with chessboard metric gives Chebyshev distance to wall\n    dist = distance_transform_cdt(~wall_mask, metric='taxicab')\n    far_from_wall = dist > 1\n    eligible = (grid == bg) & far_from_wall\n    all_coords = list(zip(*np.where(eligible)))\n    fill_coords = [p for p in all_coords if nominal_fill_zone[p[0], p[1]]]\n    random.shuffle(all_coords)\n    random.shuffle(fill_coords)\n    return [(int(r), int(c)) for r, c in all_coords], [(int(r), int(c)) for r, c in fill_coords]\n\n\ndef _place_obstacles(n_obstacles, candidates, fill_candidates, obstacle_colors, grid):\n    \"\"\"Place up to n_obstacles on the grid, preferring some in the fill zone.\"\"\"\n    placed = []\n    n_fill = random.randint(0, min(n_obstacles, len(fill_candidates)))\n    if fill_candidates and random.random() < OBSTACLE_IN_FILL_PROB:\n        n_fill = max(1, n_fill)\n    for p in fill_candidates:\n        if len(placed) >= n_fill:\n            break\n        if _obstacle_fits(p, placed):\n            placed.append(p)\n    placed_set = set(placed)\n    for p in candidates:\n        if len(placed) >= n_obstacles:\n            break\n        if p not in placed_set and _obstacle_fits(p, placed):\n            placed.append(p)\n            placed_set.add(p)\n    for i, (r, c) in enumerate(placed):\n        grid[r, c] = obstacle_colors[i % len(obstacle_colors)]\n\n\ndef _touches_border(mask):\n    \"\"\"Check if any True cell in mask lies on the grid border.\"\"\"\n    return bool(mask[0, :].any() or mask[-1, :].any() or mask[:, 0].any() or mask[:, -1].any())\n\n\ndef _gen_attempt():\n    \"\"\"Single attempt to generate a valid input grid.\"\"\"\n    rad = random.choice([(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)])\n    h = random.randint(*GRID_H_VERT) if rad[0] else random.randint(*GRID_H_HORIZ)\n    w = random.randint(*GRID_W)\n\n    built = _build_local_wall(rad, h, w)\n    if built is None:\n        return None\n    local_wall, depth, gap = built\n    anchor = _place_anchor(rad, h, w, depth, gap)\n\n    # Choose palette\n    palette = random.sample(range(10), random.randint(*PALETTE_SIZE))\n    bg, wall_color, fill_color = palette[:3]\n    obstacle_colors = palette[3:]\n\n    # Render wall onto grid\n    grid = np.full((h, w), bg, dtype=int)\n    for u, v in local_wall:\n        r, c = _map_local(u, v, rad, anchor)\n        grid[r, c] = wall_color\n\n    # Place fill seed near the closed end\n    interior = _local_interior(local_wall, depth, gap)\n    wall_set = set(local_wall)\n    adjacent = [\n        p for p in interior\n        if any((p[0] + dr, p[1] + dc) in wall_set for dr, dc in [(-1, 0), (1, 0), (0, -1), (0, 1)])\n    ]\n    seed_pool = [p for p in adjacent if p[0] >= max(1, depth - SEED_DEPTH_LOOKBACK)]\n    if not seed_pool:\n        return None\n\n    blob = _grow_seed_blob(random.choice(seed_pool), set(interior))\n    for u, v in blob:\n        r, c = _map_local(u, v, rad, anchor)\n        if grid[r, c] != bg:\n            return None\n        grid[r, c] = fill_color\n\n    # Run solver to find nominal fill zone (used for obstacle placement)\n    try:\n        nominal_out = main(grid)\n    except Exception:\n        return None\n    nominal_fill_zone = (nominal_out == fill_color) & (grid == bg)\n\n    # Place obstacles\n    n_obstacles = random.randint(0, MAX_OBSTACLES)\n    if n_obstacles and not obstacle_colors:\n        available = [c for c in range(10) if c not in (bg, wall_color, fill_color)]\n        obstacle_colors = random.sample(available, random.randint(1, 2))\n    if n_obstacles:\n        candidates, fill_candidates = _find_obstacle_candidates(grid, bg, wall_color, nominal_fill_zone)\n        _place_obstacles(n_obstacles, candidates, fill_candidates, obstacle_colors, grid)\n\n    # Validate: solver must produce enough fill that reaches a border\n    try:\n        out = main(grid)\n    except Exception:\n        return None\n    new_fill = (out == fill_color) & (grid == bg)\n    if int(new_fill.sum()) < MIN_NEW_FILL or not _touches_border(new_fill):\n        return None\n    return grid\n\n\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(GEN_RETRIES):\n        grid = _gen_attempt()\n        if grid is not None:\n            return grid\n    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate valid b9e38dc0 input\")\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport numpy as np\n\nfrom arc_utils import bbox_coords, get_bg\nimport random\n\n\ndef _legend_pairs(g: np.ndarray, bg: int) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n    pairs: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n    for r in range(g.shape[0]):\n        left = int(g[r, 0])\n        right = int(g[r, 1])\n        if left == bg or right == bg:\n            break\n        pairs.append((left, right))\n    assert pairs, \"expected a non-empty two-column legend at the upper left\"\n    return pairs\n\n\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"Expand each sparse color object into a filled rectangular region using its legend-paired fill color.\n\n    Input structure\n    A background-color grid has a two-column legend in columns 0 and 1, starting at row 0: each row gives\n    a source color in the left column and that source color's fill color in the right column.  Outside the\n    legend, the foreground pixels are sparse dot objects whose colors are exactly the legend source colors.\n\n    Rule\n    Clear the legend.  For each source color, take the bbox of all of its non-legend dots, expand that bbox\n    by one pixel in every direction, and fill background cells inside the expanded rectangle with the paired\n    legend fill color while preserving every original non-legend dot.\n\n    Key insight\n    The legend order is also the layer order: earlier legend rows are in front, so fill rectangles are painted\n    from the last legend row back to the first and higher-priority fills overwrite lower-priority fills.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    bg = get_bg(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    assert W >= 2, \"expected room for a two-column legend\"\n\n    pairs = _legend_pairs(g, bg)\n    source_colors = {source for source, _fill in pairs}\n    assert len(source_colors) == len(pairs), \"legend source colors must be unique\"\n\n    legend_mask = np.zeros(g.shape, dtype=bool)\n    legend_mask[: len(pairs), :2] = True\n    dot_mask = (g != bg) & ~legend_mask\n    assert dot_mask.any(), \"expected source dots outside the legend\"\n    assert set(map(int, g[dot_mask])).issubset(source_colors), \"outside dots must use legend source colors\"\n\n    dots_by_color: dict[int, np.ndarray] = {}\n    all_dots = np.zeros(g.shape, dtype=bool)\n    for source, _fill in pairs:\n        coords = np.argwhere(dot_mask & (g == source))\n        assert coords.size, \"each legend source color must appear outside the legend\"\n        dots_by_color[source] = coords\n        all_dots[coords[:, 0], coords[:, 1]] = True\n\n    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n    for source, fill in reversed(pairs):\n        r0, c0, r1, c1 = bbox_coords(dots_by_color[source])\n        r0 = max(0, r0 - 1)\n        c0 = max(0, c0 - 1)\n        r1 = min(H - 1, r1 + 1)\n        c1 = min(W - 1, c1 + 1)\n\n        region = out[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1]\n        background_inside_region = ~all_dots[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1]\n        region[background_inside_region] = fill\n\n    out[all_dots] = g[all_dots]\n    return out\n\n# --------------- generator (o5x) ---------------\n\n# Canvas and legend geometry.\nGRID_HEIGHT, GRID_WIDTH = 20, 20\nBACKGROUND_COLOR = 0\nLEGEND_COLS = 2\nDOT_STRIDE, BBOX_PAD, NON_OVERLAP_GAP = 2, 1, 1\n\n# Legend palette sampling.\nCOLORS = tuple(range(1, 10))\nPAIR_COUNT_CHOICES = (2, 3)\nROTATED_SOURCE_FILL_PROB = 0.45\n\n# Dot-grid shape sampling.\nDOT_ROWS_MIN, DOT_ROWS_MAX = 2, 8\nDOT_COLS_MIN, DOT_COLS_MAX = 2, 5\n\n# Layout search.\nTHREE_PAIR_FORCED_OVERLAP_STEPS = (1, 2)\nMODE_OVERLAP, MODE_OVERLAP_ANY, MODE_SEPARATE, MODE_ANY = \"overlap\", \"overlap_any\", \"separate\", \"any\"\nPOST_FORCED_LAYOUT_MODES = (MODE_SEPARATE, MODE_OVERLAP_ANY, MODE_ANY)\nPOST_FORCED_LAYOUT_WEIGHTS = (3, 3, 1)\n\n# Generation retry budget.\nGENERATION_RETRIES = 300\n\n\ndef _origin_bbox(origin: tuple[int, int], dot_rows: int, dot_cols: int) -> tuple[int, int, int, int]:\n    r, c = origin\n    return (\n        r - BBOX_PAD,\n        c - BBOX_PAD,\n        r + DOT_STRIDE * (dot_rows - 1) + BBOX_PAD,\n        c + DOT_STRIDE * (dot_cols - 1) + BBOX_PAD,\n    )\n\n\ndef _hits_legend(box: tuple[int, int, int, int], n_pairs: int) -> bool:\n    r0, c0, r1, c1 = box\n    return r0 <= n_pairs - 1 and r1 >= 0 and c0 <= LEGEND_COLS - 1 and c1 >= 0\n\n\ndef _legal_origins(n_pairs: int, dot_rows: int, dot_cols: int) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n    origins: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n    max_r = GRID_HEIGHT - 1 - BBOX_PAD - DOT_STRIDE * (dot_rows - 1)\n    max_c = GRID_WIDTH - 1 - BBOX_PAD - DOT_STRIDE * (dot_cols - 1)\n    for r in range(BBOX_PAD, max_r + 1):\n        for c in range(BBOX_PAD, max_c + 1):\n            box = _origin_bbox((r, c), dot_rows, dot_cols)\n            if not _hits_legend(box, n_pairs):\n                origins.append((r, c))\n    return origins\n\n\ndef _dot_coords(origin: tuple[int, int], dot_rows: int, dot_cols: int) -> np.ndarray:\n    rows = origin[0] + DOT_STRIDE * np.arange(dot_rows)\n    cols = origin[1] + DOT_STRIDE * np.arange(dot_cols)\n    rr, cc = np.meshgrid(rows, cols, indexing=\"ij\")\n    return np.stack([rr.ravel(), cc.ravel()], axis=1).astype(int)\n\n\ndef _expanded_bbox(coords: np.ndarray) -> tuple[int, int, int, int]:\n    r0, c0, r1, c1 = bbox_coords(coords)\n    return (\n        max(0, r0 - BBOX_PAD),\n        max(0, c0 - BBOX_PAD),\n        min(GRID_HEIGHT - 1, r1 + BBOX_PAD),\n        min(GRID_WIDTH - 1, c1 + BBOX_PAD),\n    )\n\n\ndef _inside_box(coords: np.ndarray, box: tuple[int, int, int, int]) -> np.ndarray:\n    r0, c0, r1, c1 = box\n    return (coords[:, 0] >= r0) & (coords[:, 0] <= r1) & (coords[:, 1] >= c0) & (coords[:, 1] <= c1)\n\n\ndef _separated_by_gap(a: tuple[int, int, int, int], b: tuple[int, int, int, int]) -> bool:\n    return (\n        a[2] + NON_OVERLAP_GAP < b[0]\n        or b[2] + NON_OVERLAP_GAP < a[0]\n        or a[3] + NON_OVERLAP_GAP < b[1]\n        or b[3] + NON_OVERLAP_GAP < a[1]\n    )\n\n\ndef _intersection_bounds(\n    a: tuple[int, int, int, int], b: tuple[int, int, int, int]\n) -> tuple[int, int, int, int] | None:\n    r0, c0 = max(a[0], b[0]), max(a[1], b[1])\n    r1, c1 = min(a[2], b[2]), min(a[3], b[3])\n    if r0 > r1 or c0 > c1:\n        return None\n    return r0, c0, r1, c1\n\n\ndef _intersection_has_background(\n    a: tuple[int, int, int, int], b: tuple[int, int, int, int], dot_mask: np.ndarray\n) -> bool:\n    bounds = _intersection_bounds(a, b)\n    if bounds is None:\n        return False\n    r0, c0, r1, c1 = bounds\n    return bool((~dot_mask[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1]).any())\n\n\ndef _remove_dots_covered_by_higher_priority(\n    coords: np.ndarray, higher_boxes: list[tuple[int, int, int, int]]\n) -> np.ndarray:\n    keep = np.ones(len(coords), dtype=bool)\n    for box in higher_boxes:\n        keep &= ~_inside_box(coords, box)\n    return coords[keep]\n\n\ndef _scene_data(\n    sizes: list[tuple[int, int]], origins: list[tuple[int, int]]\n) -> tuple[list[np.ndarray], list[tuple[int, int, int, int]], np.ndarray] | None:\n    remaining: list[np.ndarray] = []\n    boxes: list[tuple[int, int, int, int]] = []\n    dot_mask = np.zeros((GRID_HEIGHT, GRID_WIDTH), dtype=bool)\n\n    for (dot_rows, dot_cols), origin in zip(sizes, origins):\n        coords = _remove_dots_covered_by_higher_priority(_dot_coords(origin, dot_rows, dot_cols), boxes)\n        if coords.size == 0:\n            return None\n        if len(set(map(int, coords[:, 0]))) != dot_rows or len(set(map(int, coords[:, 1]))) != dot_cols:\n            return None\n        if dot_mask[coords[:, 0], coords[:, 1]].any():\n            return None\n        dot_mask[coords[:, 0], coords[:, 1]] = True\n        remaining.append(coords)\n        boxes.append(_expanded_bbox(coords))\n\n    return remaining, boxes, dot_mask\n\n\ndef _render_output(\n    pairs: list[tuple[int, int]], remaining: list[np.ndarray], boxes: list[tuple[int, int, int, int]], dot_mask: np.ndarray\n) -> np.ndarray:\n    out = np.full((GRID_HEIGHT, GRID_WIDTH), BACKGROUND_COLOR, dtype=int)\n    for idx in range(len(pairs) - 1, -1, -1):\n        _source, fill = pairs[idx]\n        r0, c0, r1, c1 = boxes[idx]\n        region = out[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1]\n        region[~dot_mask[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1]] = fill\n    for (source, _fill), coords in zip(pairs, remaining):\n        out[coords[:, 0], coords[:, 1]] = source\n    return out\n\n\ndef _build_input(pairs: list[tuple[int, int]], remaining: list[np.ndarray]) -> np.ndarray:\n    grid = np.full((GRID_HEIGHT, GRID_WIDTH), BACKGROUND_COLOR, dtype=int)\n    grid[: len(pairs), :LEGEND_COLS] = np.asarray(pairs, dtype=int)\n    for (source, _fill), coords in zip(pairs, remaining):\n        grid[coords[:, 0], coords[:, 1]] = source\n    return grid\n\n\ndef _visible_overlap(\n    pairs: list[tuple[int, int]], boxes: list[tuple[int, int, int, int]], dot_mask: np.ndarray, out: np.ndarray\n) -> bool:\n    for hi in range(len(pairs)):\n        for lo in range(hi + 1, len(pairs)):\n            bounds = _intersection_bounds(boxes[hi], boxes[lo])\n            if bounds is None:\n                continue\n            r0, c0, r1, c1 = bounds\n            overlap_fill = (~dot_mask[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1]) & (\n                out[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1] == pairs[hi][1]\n            )\n            lr0, lc0, lr1, lc1 = boxes[lo]\n            lower_fill = (~dot_mask[lr0 : lr1 + 1, lc0 : lc1 + 1]) & (\n                out[lr0 : lr1 + 1, lc0 : lc1 + 1] == pairs[lo][1]\n            )\n            if overlap_fill.any() and lower_fill.any():\n                return True\n    return False\n\n\ndef _choose_pairs(n_pairs: int) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n    colors = list(COLORS)\n    sources = random.sample(colors, n_pairs)\n    non_sources = [c for c in colors if c not in sources]\n    if random.random() < ROTATED_SOURCE_FILL_PROB:\n        fills = sources[1:] + sources[:1]\n        replace_count = random.randint(0, n_pairs)\n        for idx, color in zip(random.sample(range(n_pairs), replace_count), random.sample(non_sources, replace_count)):\n            fills[idx] = color\n    else:\n        fills = random.sample(non_sources, n_pairs)\n    return list(zip(sources, fills))\n\n\ndef _candidate_origins(\n    sizes: list[tuple[int, int]],\n    origins: list[tuple[int, int]],\n    legal: list[tuple[int, int]],\n    mode: str,\n    target: int | None = None,\n) -> list[tuple[int, int]]:\n    candidates: list[tuple[int, int]] = []\n    for origin in legal:\n        data = _scene_data(sizes[: len(origins) + 1], origins + [origin])\n        if data is None:\n            continue\n        _remaining, boxes, dot_mask = data\n        new_box = boxes[-1]\n        if mode == MODE_OVERLAP:\n            assert target is not None\n            if not _intersection_has_background(new_box, boxes[target], dot_mask):\n                continue\n        elif mode == MODE_OVERLAP_ANY:\n            if not any(_intersection_has_background(new_box, old_box, dot_mask) for old_box in boxes[:-1]):\n                continue\n        elif mode == MODE_SEPARATE:\n            if not all(_separated_by_gap(new_box, old_box) for old_box in boxes[:-1]):\n                continue\n        candidates.append(origin)\n    return candidates\n\n\ndef _choose_origins(n_pairs: int, sizes: list[tuple[int, int]]) -> list[tuple[int, int]] | None:\n    legal = [_legal_origins(n_pairs, dot_rows, dot_cols) for dot_rows, dot_cols in sizes]\n    if any(not options for options in legal):\n        return None\n\n    origins = [random.choice(legal[0])]\n    forced_step = 1 if n_pairs == 2 else random.choice(THREE_PAIR_FORCED_OVERLAP_STEPS)\n    for idx in range(1, n_pairs):\n        if idx == forced_step:\n            target = random.randrange(idx)\n            candidates = _candidate_origins(sizes, origins, legal[idx], MODE_OVERLAP, target)\n        elif idx < forced_step:\n            candidates = _candidate_origins(sizes, origins, legal[idx], MODE_SEPARATE)\n            if not candidates:\n                candidates = _candidate_origins(sizes, origins, legal[idx], MODE_ANY)\n        else:\n            mode = random.choices(POST_FORCED_LAYOUT_MODES, weights=POST_FORCED_LAYOUT_WEIGHTS, k=1)[0]\n            candidates = _candidate_origins(sizes, origins, legal[idx], mode)\n            if not candidates:\n                candidates = _candidate_origins(sizes, origins, legal[idx], MODE_ANY)\n        if not candidates:\n            return None\n        origins.append(random.choice(candidates))\n    return origins\n\n\ndef _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\n    n_pairs = random.choice(PAIR_COUNT_CHOICES)\n    pairs = _choose_pairs(n_pairs)\n    sizes = [\n        (random.randint(DOT_ROWS_MIN, DOT_ROWS_MAX), random.randint(DOT_COLS_MIN, DOT_COLS_MAX))\n        for _ in range(n_pairs)\n    ]\n    origins = _choose_origins(n_pairs, sizes)\n    if origins is None:\n        return None\n\n    data = _scene_data(sizes, origins)\n    if data is None:\n        return None\n    remaining, boxes, dot_mask = data\n    out = _render_output(pairs, remaining, boxes, dot_mask)\n    if not _visible_overlap(pairs, boxes, dot_mask, out):\n        return None\n\n    inp = _build_input(pairs, remaining)\n    if not np.array_equal(main(inp), out):\n        return None\n    return inp\n\n\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(GENERATION_RETRIES):\n        grid = _gen_attempt()\n        if grid is not None:\n            return grid\n    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate a valid 305b1341 input\")\nfrom __future__ import annotations\n\nimport numpy as np\n\nfrom arc_utils import Color, colors_present, find_components, get_bg, is_contiguous, paste\nimport itertools\nimport math\nimport random\n\n\nRED = Color.RED\nGREEN = Color.GREEN\n\n\ndef _component_mask(coords: np.ndarray, bbox: tuple[int, int, int, int]) -> np.ndarray:\n    r0, c0, r1, c1 = bbox\n    mask = np.zeros((r1 - r0 + 1, c1 - c0 + 1), dtype=bool)\n    mask[coords[:, 0] - r0, coords[:, 1] - c0] = True\n    return mask\n\n\ndef _has_flanked_boundary_gap(mask: np.ndarray) -> bool:\n    h, w = mask.shape\n\n    for c in range(w):\n        if not mask[0, c] and mask[0, :c].any() and mask[0, c + 1 :].any():\n            return True\n        if h > 1 and not mask[h - 1, c] and mask[h - 1, :c].any() and mask[h - 1, c + 1 :].any():\n            return True\n\n    for r in range(h):\n        if not mask[r, 0] and mask[:r, 0].any() and mask[r + 1 :, 0].any():\n            return True\n        if w > 1 and not mask[r, w - 1] and mask[:r, w - 1].any() and mask[r + 1 :, w - 1].any():\n            return True\n\n    return False\n\n\ndef main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n    \"\"\"\n    Slide each foreground object horizontally to the edge indicated by whether its shape has an open notch.\n\n    Input structure:\n    - Yellow is the background and there is one blue foreground color.\n    - The foreground is split into separated 4-connected objects.\n    - Each object keeps its row span and shape in the output.\n\n    Rule:\n    - If an object's bbox border contains a background gap flanked by object pixels along that same border, slide it to the right edge and recolor it green.\n    - Otherwise slide the object to the left edge and recolor it red.\n    - Start from a blank background canvas; no moved objects overlap.\n\n    Key insight:\n    - The target side is encoded by the object's shape, not by its original position: open notches go right, while closed or solid shapes go left.\n    \"\"\"\n    g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n    bg = int(get_bg(g))\n    fg_colors = colors_present(g, exclude={bg})\n    assert len(fg_colors) == 1, \"expected exactly one foreground color\"\n    fg = int(fg_colors[0])\n\n    components = find_components(g, bg=bg, connectivity=4, monochromatic=True, colors=[fg])\n    assert components, \"expected at least one foreground object\"\n\n    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n    W = g.shape[1]\n    for component in components:\n        bbox = tuple(map(int, component[\"bbox\"]))\n        r0, _c0, r1, _c1 = bbox\n        mask = _component_mask(component[\"coords\"], bbox)\n        h, w = mask.shape\n\n        has_open_notch = _has_flanked_boundary_gap(mask)\n        dst_c = W - w if has_open_notch else 0\n        color = GREEN if has_open_notch else RED\n        assert 0 <= dst_c and dst_c + w <= W, \"moved object must fit within the grid\"\n\n        dst = out[r0 : r1 + 1, dst_c : dst_c + w]\n        assert dst.shape == (h, w), \"destination slice must match object bbox\"\n        assert np.all(dst[mask] == bg), \"moved objects must not overlap\"\n        dst[mask] = color\n\n    return out\n\n# --------------- generator (o5x) ---------------\n\n# Canvas and colors.\nBG, BLUE, RED, GREEN = Color.YELLOW, Color.BLUE, Color.RED, Color.GREEN\nGRID_H, GRID_W_MIN, GRID_W_MAX = 22, 19, 26\n\n# Object counts, dimensions, and row separation.\nOBJECT_COUNT_MIN, OBJECT_COUNT_MAX, NOTCH_OBJECT_COUNT = 3, 5, 2\nOBJECT_H_MIN, OBJECT_H_MAX, OBJECT_W_MIN, OBJECT_W_MAX = 2, 6, 2, 9\nMIN_ROW_GAP = 1\n\n# Shape density and carving probabilities.\nMIN_FILL_RATIO, CLOSED_LARGE_AREA_MIN = 0.64, 12\nNOTCH_INTERIOR_CARVE_PROB, EXTRA_CARVE_PROB = 0.45, 0.20\nCLOSED_CARVE_PROB_SMALL, CLOSED_CARVE_PROB_LARGE = 0.45, 0.88\n\n# Placement and retry limits.\nINTERIOR_COL_PAD, MAX_FOREGROUND_FRACTION = 1, 0.5\nGENERATION_RETRIES = 300\n\n\ndef _has_flanked_boundary_gap(mask: np.ndarray) -> bool:\n    h, w = mask.shape\n    for c in range(w):\n        if not mask[0, c] and mask[0, :c].any() and mask[0, c + 1 :].any():\n            return True\n        if h > 1 and not mask[h - 1, c] and mask[h - 1, :c].any() and mask[h - 1, c + 1 :].any():\n            return True\n    for r in range(h):\n        if not mask[r, 0] and mask[:r, 0].any() and mask[r + 1 :, 0].any():\n            return True\n        if w > 1 and not mask[r, w - 1] and mask[:r, w - 1].any() and mask[r + 1 :, w - 1].any():\n            return True\n    return False\n\n\ndef _patch(mask: np.ndarray, color: int) -> np.ndarray:\n    out = np.full(mask.shape, BG, dtype=int)\n    out[mask] = int(color)\n    return out\n\n\ndef _random_heights(n: int) -> list[int]:\n    usable = GRID_H - MIN_ROW_GAP * (n - 1)\n    choices = [\n        hs\n        for hs in itertools.product(range(OBJECT_H_MIN, OBJECT_H_MAX + 1), repeat=n)\n        if sum(hs) <= usable\n    ]\n    return list(random.choice(choices))\n\n\ndef _row_starts(heights: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n    free = GRID_H - sum(heights) - MIN_ROW_GAP * (len(heights) - 1)\n    gaps = [0] * (len(heights) + 1)\n    for _ in range(free):\n        gaps[random.randrange(len(gaps))] += 1\n\n    starts = []\n    r = gaps[0]\n    for i, h in enumerate(heights):\n        starts.append(r)\n        r += h\n        if i + 1 < len(heights):\n            r += MIN_ROW_GAP + gaps[i + 1]\n    return starts\n\n\ndef _carve_interior_rect(mask: np.ndarray, max_remove: int, probability: float) -> int:\n    if max_remove <= 0 or random.random() > probability:\n        return 0\n\n    h, w = mask.shape\n    choices = []\n    for r0 in range(1, h - 1):\n        for c0 in range(1, w - 1):\n            for r1 in range(r0, h - 1):\n                for c1 in range(c0, w - 1):\n                    area = (r1 - r0 + 1) * (c1 - c0 + 1)\n                    if area <= max_remove and np.all(mask[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1]):\n                        choices.append((r0, c0, r1, c1, area))\n    if not choices:\n        return 0\n\n    r0, c0, r1, c1, area = random.choice(choices)\n    mask[r0 : r1 + 1, c0 : c1 + 1] = False\n    return int(area)\n\n\ndef _make_notch_mask(h: int, w: int) -> np.ndarray | None:\n    area = h * w\n    max_remove = area - math.ceil(area * MIN_FILL_RATIO)\n    edges = []\n    if w >= OBJECT_W_MIN + 1:\n        edges.extend([\"top\", \"bottom\"])\n    if h >= OBJECT_H_MIN + 1:\n        edges.extend([\"left\", \"right\"])\n    if not edges or max_remove < 1:\n        return None\n\n    mask = np.ones((h, w), dtype=bool)\n    edge = random.choice(edges)\n    edge_len = w if edge in {\"top\", \"bottom\"} else h\n    run = random.randint(1, min(edge_len - 2, max_remove))\n    start = random.randint(1, edge_len - run - 1)\n\n    if edge in {\"top\", \"bottom\"}:\n        depth = random.randint(1, min(h - 1, max_remove // run))\n        if edge == \"top\":\n            mask[:depth, start : start + run] = False\n        else:\n            mask[h - depth :, start : start + run] = False\n    elif edge == \"left\":\n        depth = random.randint(1, min(w - 1, max_remove // run))\n        mask[start : start + run, :depth] = False\n    else:\n        depth = random.randint(1, min(w - 1, max_remove // run))\n        mask[start : start + run, w - depth :] = False\n\n    removed = run * depth\n    removed += _carve_interior_rect(mask, max_remove - removed, NOTCH_INTERIOR_CARVE_PROB)\n    _carve_interior_rect(mask, max_remove - removed, EXTRA_CARVE_PROB)\n    if not _has_flanked_boundary_gap(mask):\n        return None\n    if not is_contiguous(mask.astype(int), background=0, connectivity=4):\n        return None\n    return mask\n\n\ndef _make_closed_mask(h: int, w: int) -> np.ndarray | None:\n    area = h * w\n    mask = np.ones((h, w), dtype=bool)\n    max_remove = area - math.ceil(area * MIN_FILL_RATIO)\n    probability = CLOSED_CARVE_PROB_LARGE if area >= CLOSED_LARGE_AREA_MIN else CLOSED_CARVE_PROB_SMALL\n    removed = _carve_interior_rect(mask, max_remove, probability)\n    _carve_interior_rect(mask, max_remove - removed, EXTRA_CARVE_PROB)\n    if _has_flanked_boundary_gap(mask):\n        return None\n    if not is_contiguous(mask.astype(int), background=0, connectivity=4):\n        return None\n    return mask\n\n\ndef _sample_layout() -> tuple[int, list[str], list[int], list[int]]:\n    W = random.randint(GRID_W_MIN, GRID_W_MAX)\n    n = random.randint(OBJECT_COUNT_MIN, OBJECT_COUNT_MAX)\n    kinds = [\"notch\"] * NOTCH_OBJECT_COUNT + [\"closed\"] * (n - NOTCH_OBJECT_COUNT)\n    random.shuffle(kinds)\n\n    heights = _random_heights(n)\n    starts = _row_starts(heights)\n    return W, kinds, heights, starts\n\n\ndef _sample_objects(grid_w: int, kinds: list[str], starts: list[int], heights: list[int]):\n    max_w = min(OBJECT_W_MAX, (grid_w - 1) // 2)\n\n    objects = []\n    for kind, r0, h in zip(kinds, starts, heights):\n        if kind == \"notch\":\n            widths = [w for w in range(OBJECT_W_MIN, max_w + 1) if w >= 3 or h >= 3]\n            w = random.choice(widths)\n            mask = _make_notch_mask(h, w)\n            color, edge_c = GREEN, grid_w - w\n        else:\n            w = random.randint(OBJECT_W_MIN, max_w)\n            mask = _make_closed_mask(h, w)\n            color, edge_c = RED, 0\n        if mask is None:\n            return None\n        objects.append((mask, r0, w, color, edge_c))\n    return objects\n\n\ndef _render_pair(objects, grid_w: int) -> tuple[np.ndarray, np.ndarray] | None:\n    inp = np.full((GRID_H, grid_w), BG, dtype=int)\n    out = np.full((GRID_H, grid_w), BG, dtype=int)\n    for mask, r0, w, color, edge_c in objects:\n        paste(out, _patch(mask, color), r0, edge_c, transparent=BG)\n\n    if sum(int(mask.sum()) for mask, *_ in objects) >= (GRID_H * grid_w) * MAX_FOREGROUND_FRACTION:\n        return None\n\n    for mask, r0, w, _color, _edge_c in objects:\n        c0 = random.randint(INTERIOR_COL_PAD, grid_w - w - INTERIOR_COL_PAD)\n        paste(inp, _patch(mask, BLUE), r0, c0, transparent=BG)\n    return inp, out\n\n\ndef _oracle_accepts(inp: np.ndarray, out: np.ndarray) -> bool:\n    try:\n        solved = main(inp)\n    except AssertionError:\n        return False\n    return bool(np.array_equal(solved, out))\n\n\ndef _gen_attempt() -> np.ndarray | None:\n    W, kinds, heights, starts = _sample_layout()\n    objects = _sample_objects(W, kinds, starts, heights)\n    if objects is None:\n        return None\n\n    rendered = _render_pair(objects, W)\n    if rendered is None:\n        return None\n\n    inp, out = rendered\n    return inp if _oracle_accepts(inp, out) else None\n\n\ndef generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n    for _ in range(GENERATION_RETRIES):\n        grid = _gen_attempt()\n        if grid is not None:\n            return grid\n    raise RuntimeError(\"failed to generate valid 320afe60 input\")\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：根据每个封闭区域内部散落标记像素的颜色，从外部调色板中选取对应形状模板，将模板缩放后填入该封闭区域。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为一种主色（如 black）。\n  - 网格中有若干由同一颜色（如 blue）绘制的不规则封闭框，每个框的内部面积不同。\n  - 每个封闭框内部散落着 1-3 个同色标记像素（颜色各不相同，如 red、green、yellow 等）。\n  - 网格的底部或右侧有一排\"调色板区域\"：每种标记颜色对应一个小型形状模板（如 3x3 的 L 形、T 形、十字形等），模板由该标记颜色绘制在背景上。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 对每个封闭框，统计其内部标记像素的数量 N。\n  - 在该封闭框内部所有标记像素中，找到出现次数最多的颜色（保证唯一）。\n  - 在调色板区域找到与该颜色匹配的形状模板。\n  - 将模板整体缩放 N 倍（每个模板像素变成 NxN 的方块）。\n  - 将缩放后的模板居中放置在封闭框内部（框的内部空间恰好能容纳缩放后的模板）。\n  - 封闭框本身和调色板区域保持不变。\n\n- `Key insight`：标记像素同时承担两个角色——其颜色选择模板形状，其数量决定缩放倍率。解题者需要先发现\"颜色选模板\"的对应关系，再发现\"计数定缩放\"的第二层规则。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：将网格中散落的彩色碎片按遮挡关系还原为完整矩形，并按从上层到下层的顺序水平排列输出。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为一种主色（如 black）。\n  - 网格中有若干不同颜色的前景像素区域，它们是多个矩形层叠后的可见部分。\n  - 每个矩形原本是纯色实心矩形，但由于相互遮挡，部分矩形只露出 L 形、条形或不规则碎片。\n  - 每种前景颜色恰好对应一个矩形。\n  - 至少有一个矩形完全可见（未被遮挡），其完整形状直接呈现。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 识别每种前景颜色的所有像素，推断其原始矩形的完整边界（即包含该颜色所有像素的最小外接矩形）。\n  - 确定遮挡关系：若颜色 A 的外接矩形范围内出现了颜色 B 的像素，则 B 在 A 之上。\n  - 根据遮挡关系进行拓扑排序，从最上层到最下层排列。\n  - 输出网格：将每个颜色的完整矩形（用其颜色填充）从左到右依次水平排列，矩形之间间隔一列背景色，顺序为从最上层（左）到最下层（右）。\n  - 输出网格的高度等于所有矩形高度的最大值，不足的矩形在底部用背景色填充。\n\n- `Key insight`：可见碎片的形状本身不是答案——解题者需要反推每个矩形被遮挡前的完整尺寸，再通过\"谁覆盖了谁\"建立层叠顺序。两个推理层缺一不可：还原完整形状是第一层，确定层叠排序是第二层。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：在分隔线划分的子区域中，根据每个区域内\"种子像素\"的颜色找到对应的旋转模板，再根据种子像素相对于区域中心的象限位置确定旋转角度后填入。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为一种主色（如 black）。\n  - 网格被一种分隔色（如 grey）的完整行和完整列划分为大小相同的正方形子区域（如 5x5）。\n  - 网格左上角的子区域包含若干\"模板定义\"：每个模板由一种非背景非分隔色绘制的小图案组成（如 red 的箭头形、green 的阶梯形），模板本身不具有四重旋转对称性。\n  - 其余子区域中，每个子区域要么为空（纯背景），要么在区域内恰好有一个\"种子像素\"——一个非背景非分隔色的单像素点。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 识别左上角区域中定义的各颜色模板及其原始朝向。\n  - 对每个含种子像素的子区域：\n    - 种子像素的颜色决定使用哪个模板。\n    - 种子像素相对于该子区域中心点的位置决定旋转角度：左上象限 = 0 度（原始朝向）、右上象限 = 顺时针 90 度、右下象限 = 顺时针 180 度、左下象限 = 顺时针 270 度。\n  - 将旋转后的模板居中绘制在该子区域内，使用种子像素的颜色。\n  - 种子像素本身被模板覆盖。空子区域保持不变。\n\n- `Key insight`：种子像素编码了两层信息——颜色选择模板形状，空间位置（象限）选择旋转角度。解题者容易注意到颜色-模板的对应关系，但需要进一步发现位置-旋转的隐含编码才能完成推理。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：识别网格中由不同颜色绘制的有向路径箭头，沿箭头指向传递颜色，每个对象最终显示为其上游箭头指向它时所携带的颜色。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为一种主色（如 black）。\n  - 网格中有若干彩色对象，每个对象由两部分组成：一个 3x3 的实心正方形\"头部\"和一条从头部某一侧中央延伸出的长度为 2 的单像素宽\"尾巴\"。头部和尾巴同色。\n  - 整个对象构成一个箭头形状：尾巴所在的方向是箭头的来源方向，头部对面的方向是箭头的指向方向。例如尾巴在左侧，则箭头指向右方。\n  - 每个箭头的颜色各不相同。\n  - 箭头的指向方向上（沿水平或垂直直线延伸）恰好会遇到另一个箭头的头部侧面。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 识别每个箭头对象及其指向方向（头部对侧为尾巴所在方向，指向方向为尾巴的反方向）。\n  - 建立指向关系链：箭头 A 指向箭头 B，意味着从 A 的头部沿指向方向直线前进最先碰到的对象是 B。\n  - 找到链的起点（没有被任何箭头指向的箭头），其颜色保持不变。\n  - 沿指向链依次传递：链中第 2 个箭头的输出颜色变为第 1 个箭头的颜色，第 3 个变为第 2 个的原始颜色，依此类推。\n  - 每个箭头（头部 + 尾巴）整体被重新着色，形状和位置不变。\n\n- `Key insight`：解题者首先需要从不对称的形状中识别出箭头的指向方向（尾巴标识来源方向，而非指向方向），然后需要发现颜色沿指向链\"后移一位\"的传递规则。方向识别是第一层推理，颜色移位是第二层推理。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：将网格中每个彩色对象沿其自身对称轴方向投影到网格边界，投影长度等于该对象在垂直于对称轴方向上的像素跨度。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为一种主色（如 black）。\n  - 网格中有若干不同颜色的前景对象，每个对象是 4-连通的。\n  - 每个对象恰好具有一条对称轴——要么是水平对称轴，要么是垂直对称轴（不会同时具有两条，也不会没有）。\n  - 对象之间不接触，且与网格边界之间有足够的空白空间。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 对每个前景对象，判断其对称轴方向（水平或垂直）。\n  - 计算该对象在垂直于对称轴的方向上的像素跨度 S（例如对称轴为水平轴，则计算该对象的列跨度）。\n  - 从该对象的外接矩形边缘出发，沿对称轴方向向最近的网格边界延伸，绘制一条长度为 S 的射线（用该对象的颜色绘制，宽度为 1 像素）。\n  - 射线起点为对象外接矩形在射线方向上最近边界一侧的中心像素。\n  - 原始对象保持不变。\n\n- `Key insight`：解题者需要完成三步非平凡推理：（1）识别每个对象的对称轴类型（水平还是垂直），（2）发现射线方向与对称轴方向一致而非垂直，（3）发现射线长度等于对象在另一个方向上的跨度而非对称轴方向的跨度。对称轴同时编码了投影方向和测量维度的选择。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：根据网格边缘色带的颜色顺序，对网格内部的同心矩形嵌套图案进行颜色层级重排。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为一种主色（如 black）。\n  - 网格中央有一个由多种颜色组成的同心矩形嵌套图案：最外层是一种颜色的单像素宽矩形框，内部是另一种颜色的矩形框，依此类推，最内层可能是一个实心小矩形。每层颜色各不相同。\n  - 网格的最上方一行（或最左方一列）有一排单像素色带，从左到右（或从上到下）依次排列若干颜色，这些颜色恰好是同心矩形中出现的所有颜色，但顺序与同心矩形中从外到内的顺序不同。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 读取边缘色带中颜色的排列顺序，从左到右记为 C1, C2, C3, ...\n  - 在原始同心矩形中，识别每层矩形框的颜色及其层级（从外到内）。\n  - 重新构建同心矩形：最外层使用 C1 的颜色，第二层使用 C2 的颜色，依此类推。每层保持单像素宽矩形框的形状，嵌套结构与原始图案的层数和几何比例完全相同，仅颜色重新分配。\n  - 边缘色带本身在输出中保持不变。\n\n- `Key insight`：同心矩形的层级结构是\"骨架\"，色带是\"重排指令\"。解题者需要先识别出同心矩形的层级顺序，再发现色带编码了新的颜色分配映射——色带的第 k 个颜色映射到第 k 层，而不是映射到某个特定颜色。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n<new_puzzle>\n- `description`：在网格中找到被噪声像素部分污染的重复图案瓷砖，利用多份副本的交叉验证恢复无噪声的原始瓷砖，并用干净瓷砖替换所有副本。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 网格由一种小型矩形瓷砖（如 4x5）平铺而成，瓷砖之间无间隔，完美覆盖整个网格。\n  - 原始瓷砖包含背景色和若干前景色像素组成的图案。\n  - 每份瓷砖副本中，有 0-3 个像素被随机替换为一种特殊\"噪声色\"（如 maroon），该噪声色不出现在原始瓷砖图案中。\n  - 不同副本中被污染的像素位置不同，但噪声颜色相同。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 确定瓷砖的尺寸（通过观察网格的周期性重复模式）。\n  - 将网格按瓷砖尺寸切分为多份副本。\n  - 对于瓷砖中的每个像素位置，统计所有副本在该位置上出现的非噪声色颜色（即排除噪声色后的众数）。\n  - 用恢复出的干净瓷砖替换网格中的所有副本，噪声像素被原始颜色替代。\n\n- `Key insight`：解题者需要先识别出平铺的周期性来确定瓷砖边界（第一层），然后发现噪声色是唯一在不同副本中出现在不同位置的颜色（第二层），最后通过交叉对比多份副本来投票恢复每个被污染像素的真实颜色（第三层）。\n</new_puzzle>\n你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1d61978c\" lines=\"8-22\">\n<description>\nRecolor the two diagonal foreground-bar orientations, assigning teal to the larger orientation and red to the smaller one.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has one dominant background color and one foreground color.\n- Foreground pixels form one-cell-thick diagonal bars.\n- Every foreground pixel belongs to exactly one diagonal orientation.\n\nRule\n- Keep the background unchanged.\n- Classify each foreground pixel by whether it has a diagonal neighbor on the NW/SE axis or the NE/SW axis.\n- Recolor the orientation with more foreground pixels teal and the other orientation red.\n\nKey insight\n- The output colors depend on the relative population of the two diagonal orientations, not on a fixed slash direction or position.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2ba387bc\" lines=\"6-21\">\n<description>\nSort scattered 4×4 squares into two columns by type: frames left, solids right.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Multiple 4×4 objects on bg.\n- Frames: colored 1px border, bg-filled 2×2 interior.\n- Solids: entirely filled with one non-bg color.\n\nRule:\n- Classify each 4×4 object as frame or solid by its interior.\n- Sort each group top-to-bottom by input row.\n- Stack frames in left column (cols 0–3), solids in right (cols 4–7).\n- Output is 8 wide; height = 4 × max(#frames, #solids), bg-padded.\n\nKey insight: Frame vs solid is determined solely by whether the 2×2 interior matches bg.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"52364a65\" lines=\"10-24\">\n<description>\nErase the two leftmost columns of every foreground object, leaving the rest of each object in place.\n\nInput structure:\n- A grid has one dominant background color and several monochromatic 4-connected foreground objects.\n- Each foreground object spans at least two columns in its bounding box.\n\nRule:\n- Find each same-color 4-connected foreground object.\n- Within each object's own bounding box, set foreground pixels in the two leftmost columns to the background color.\n- Leave all other pixels unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The deletion is measured from each object's local left edge, not from the grid edge or from a shared global column.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"8e5c0c38\" lines=\"9-21\">\n<description>\nClean each color layer by enforcing vertical mirror symmetry.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Several non-bg colors, each forming a nearly-symmetric pattern about some vertical axis.\n- Some pixels in each color are \"noise\" that breaks the symmetry.\n\nRule:\n- For each non-bg color, find the vertical axis that best explains its pixel layout.\n- Remove any pixel that lacks a same-color mirror counterpart across that axis.\n\nKey insight: Each color has its own independent symmetry axis; the task is per-color denoising, not whole-grid symmetry.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e729b7be\" lines=\"8-20\">\n<description>\nComplete the scene by adding the point-reflected copy of the colored object across the central marker.\n\nInput structure\n- The grid has odd dimensions, a dominant background, and a center-row marker color appearing only at the left edge, center, and right edge.\n- A single divider color fills the center column except for the center marker.\n- All remaining foreground pixels form the object to copy, entirely on the left side of the divider.\nRule\n- Keep the input unchanged.\n- For every object pixel, paint the pixel rotated 180 degrees around the center marker with the same color.\nKey insight\n- The center marker is the rotation center; the yellow-looking divider is not mirrored, it only separates the source from the empty target side.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"fc10701f\" lines=\"20-35\">\n<description>\nMove the orange rectangle onto the maroon rectangle and mark gated corridor crossings in red.\n\nInput structure\n- The dominant background contains black obstacle segments.\n- There is one solid orange rectangle and one solid maroon rectangle of the same size.\n- The two rectangles share either their row span or their column span, so the move is straight and axis-aligned.\n\nRule\n- Erase the original orange rectangle to the background.\n- Replace the maroon rectangle with the orange rectangle.\n- Between the two rectangles, paint red across the moving rectangle's width or height only at corridor cells flanked by black obstacle segments perpendicular to the movement.\n\nKey insight\n- The black segments define gates in the straight corridor; only those gated openings, not every swept background cell, become red.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"6165ea8f\" lines=\"74-90\">\n<description>\nBuild a color-labeled shape-equivalence table from the objects listed on the right edge.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background contains one main 4-connected object for each foreground color.\n- The rightmost column has one singleton pixel of each foreground color, listed top-to-bottom.\n- Those singleton pixels define the row and column order of the output table.\n\nRule:\n- Normalize each main object to its tight mask and compare masks up to rotation or reflection.\n- Draw 2x2 color labels across the top and down the left, separated by one background row/column.\n- For each off-diagonal table cell, paint a 2x2 red block when the two labeled objects have the same shape under rotation/reflection, otherwise paint a 2x2 grey block.\n- Leave diagonal cells and all separators as background.\n\nKey insight:\n- Object positions and original orientations are distractors; the relation is dihedral shape equivalence, while the right-edge legend fixes the table order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"97d7923e\" lines=\"81-94\">\n<description>\nRecolor vertical bars by legend-driven rank selection.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Top-left: monochrome blocks touching row 0; each has color K and height k.\n- Main grid: vertical bars capped top and bottom by a single color, with solid-color interiors.\n\nRule:\n- For each legend entry (K, k), find vertical bars capped by K.\n- Rank bars by interior length (longest = rank 1).\n- Recolor the rank-k bar's interior to K.\n\nKey insight: Legend height is a rank index into length-sorted bars, not a literal size.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"4c416de3\" lines=\"99-118\">\n<description>\nReplicate a pre-drawn corner pattern to every other marker-bearing corner.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most frequent color. Hollow 0-frames (possibly partial L-shapes); each\n  frame-corner cell is either 0 or a painted (non-bg, non-0) color.\n- >= 1 'source' corner: the pattern is already drawn (one color, both outward\n  cells and an inward-diagonal trail).\n- Other corners: a single colored pixel on the inward diagonal at the pattern's\n  deepest (k,k) offset; the pixel's color is that corner's target color.\n\nRule:\n- Extract the canonical (TL-oriented) pattern from any source.\n- For each non-source corner whose marker cell is colored, paint the same\n  pattern rotated to that corner's orientation, using the marker's color.\n\nKey insight: the pattern is symmetric about the corner's inward-diagonal axis\n(canonical dr == dc), so it reflects cleanly across all four corner orientations.\nPainting uses each target's OWN marker color, not the source color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"7e4d4f7c\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nCompress the L-shaped cue to three rows and add a pink copy of the top-row foreground mask.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color, a two-color top row, and all\nforeground pixels below the top row lie in the first column as vertical markers.\n\nRule\nKeep the input top row as output row 0 and the first marker row as output row 1.\nOutput row 2 is background except at the top-row foreground columns, which are\nrecolored pink.\n\nKey insight\nThe lower repeated marker rows only identify the vertical cue; the generated\nrow is a recolored mask of the horizontal cue in the top row.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"3e6067c3\" lines=\"7-20\">\n<description>\nConnect framed squares via colored bridges following a control-pixel sequence.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = background. Border color forms equal-sized framed squares with unique inner fill colors.\n- Control row below squares: colored pixels read left-to-right encode visitation order.\n\nRule:\n- Start at the square matching first control color c0.\n- For each consecutive pair (ci, ci+1), find the ci+1-colored square aligned in the same row or column band, preferring nearest.\n- Draw a bridge colored ci between the pair, spanning the gap with inner-square thickness.\n\nKey insight: Row/column-band alignment plus proximity resolves ambiguity when inner colors repeat.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"2d0172a1\" lines=\"209-224\">\n<description>\nConvert a rough nested line drawing into a clean canonical frame-and-dot diagram.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one background color and one foreground color.\n- Foreground 4-connected objects are rough closed frames or small filled blobs.\n- Frames may contain other frames/blobs; blobs outside a frame remain outside it.\n\nRule:\n- Detect the containment tree of foreground objects using holes of each frame object.\n- Replace every blob with one foreground pixel.\n- Replace every frame with a rectangular border padded two cells around its direct children.\n- Place siblings by their original row/column overlap and order, with one empty cell between canonical objects.\n\nKey insight: The exact wobbly geometry is irrelevant; only enclosure plus relative sibling positions define the output.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"898e7135\" lines=\"7-20\">\n<description>\nInflate a frame's hole-layout and fill each hole with its matching piece.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Largest component is a monochrome frame enclosing bg holes.\n- Outside pieces composed of k×k blocks; k = side of smallest solid square piece.\n\nRule:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
